{"name":"Medicine Man","hex":"#69556d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"medicine man","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A Native American shamanistic healer.","A traditional healer among other indigenous or ancient peoples."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/medicine_man"}],"entries":[{"title":"Medicine Man (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Medicine Man is a 1992 American adventure drama film directed by John McTiernan. The film stars Sean Connery and Lorraine Bracco as Robert Campbell and Rae Crane, biochemists working in the Amazon rainforest on a cancer cure, and features an acclaimed score by veteran composer Jerry Goldsmith."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/8b/Medicine_man.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medicine_Man_(film)","wikidata":"Q1632217"},{"title":"Medicine Man (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Medicine Man\" is a song by American rapper-producer Dr. Dre from his third studio album Compton. It was released as the album's fifteenth track on August 7, 2015 via Aftermath/Interscope Records along with the rest of the album. Recording sessions took place at Record One in Sherman Oaks and at Effigy Studios in Michigan. Produced by Dem Jointz and Focus..., the song features vocals from American rapper Eminem, South African singer Candice Pillay, and American recording artist Anderson .Paak, with additional vocals from Sly Jordan."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medicine_Man_(song)","wikidata":"Q21775815"},{"title":"Folk healer","blocks":[{"text":"A folk healer is an unlicensed person who practices the art of healing using traditional practices, herbal remedies and the power of suggestion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Curandera_performing_a_limpieza.jpg/330px-Curandera_performing_a_limpieza.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Folk_healer","wikidata":"Q2518689"},{"title":"Medicine","blocks":[{"text":"Medicine is the science and practice of caring for patients, managing the diagnosis, prognosis, prevention, treatment and palliation of their injury or disease, while promoting their health. Medicine encompasses a variety of health care practices which evolved to maintain and restore health through the prevention and treatment of illness and infection. Contemporary medicine applies biomedical sciences, biomedical research, genetics, and medical technology to diagnose, treat, and prevent injury and disease, typically through various pharmaceuticals or surgery, but also through therapies such as psychotherapy, external splints and traction, medical devices, biologics, and ionizing radiation, amongst others."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/The_Doctor_Luke_Fildes_crop.jpg/330px-The_Doctor_Luke_Fildes_crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medicine","wikidata":"Q11190"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Elizabeth_Blackwell.jpg/250px-Elizabeth_Blackwell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e7/Elizabeth_Blackwell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Elizabeth_Blackwell.jpg/250px-Elizabeth_Blackwell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Elizabeth Blackwell, the first female physician in the United States graduated from SUNY Upstate (1847)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AElizabeth_Blackwell.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Pellegrinaio_Santa_Maria_della_Scala_n5.jpg/500px-Pellegrinaio_Santa_Maria_della_Scala_n5.jpg?